WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
In first aid, shock isn't just about being surprised.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Shock is a critical, life-threatening state where the body isn't getting the blood flow and oxygen it needs.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.000
If nothing is done, shock can lead to vital organ failure and death.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
It can be triggered by severe bleeding, serious infections, heart failure, or even a major allergic reaction or spinal injury.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
As an everyday hero, you need to be able to spot the signs of shock quickly.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:35.000
The person might feel weak, dizzy, or lightheaded, and their skin often becomes cool, pale, and clammy.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:43.000
You might also notice they seem confused, have a weak but rapid pulse, or are taking very rapid, shallow breaths.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.000
If you suspect someone is going into shock, your first move is to call 911.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
While you wait for professional help, have the person lie down flat on their back.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000
If they aren't having any trouble breathing, you can prop their feet up about 12 inches to help blood flow back toward their heart and brain.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Because people in shock have trouble maintaining their body temperature, cover them with a blanket to keep them warm and calm.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Your final tasks are to ensure any obvious external bleeding is controlled with direct pressure and to stay right by their side.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Monitor their breathing and responsiveness closely.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
If they ever stop responding and stop breathing normally, you must be ready to start CPR immediately.

